 "grammar": "; Integer addition — the grammar shared with the @tabnas/parser README,\n; where it is also built by hand directly against the engine.\n;\n; Exercises two dialect features whose whole point is that this grammar\n; renders back out of a live engine exactly as written (see\n; test/roundtrip.test.js):\n;\n;   - `NR = <number>` is RFC 5234 prose-val. Prose describes a terminal\n;     rather than defining one, which for a built-in lexer token is\n;     exactly right: the lexer already supplies `NR`, so the line\n;     documents the terminal and compiles to nothing.\n;\n;   - `PL = \"+\"` is a production whose whole body is one literal, i.e. a\n;     lexical definition. It compiles to the named fixed token `#PL`\n;     rather than to a rule, matching the hand-written grammar's\n;     `fixed: { token: { '#PL': '+' } }`.\n;\n; `val` is a pure alias for `add`; it survives compilation rather than\n; being inlined away, so it is still a rule (and an AST node) here.\nval = add\nadd = NR [ PL add ]\n\nNR = <number>\nPL = \"+\"\n"
}